{0 Building the layer from scratch}

The second of two pages about how this engine is made. The first,
{{!page-"building-the-engine"} Building the engine from scratch}, rebuilds the
raycaster: rays against wall segments, a floor cast per pixel, portals drawn
recursively into an opening. It ends with a renderer you can point at a
{!Camlcast_core.World} and a loop you can drive one with.

This page covers what sits over that. A game does not build a
{!Camlcast_core.World}; it {e describes} one, every frame, from nothing, and a
runtime works out what changed. That runtime is about four hundred lines and
contains nothing engine-specific. This page rebuilds it in the order the
problems arrive.

To {e use} the layer rather than build it, read
{{!page-"making-a-game"} Making a game on CamlCast} instead.

{1 The problem, stated once}

A raycaster wants a [World]: an array of rooms, each an array of walls, joined
by portals carrying transforms. Building one is verbose to write but cheap to
run: twenty microseconds for a hundred and forty-five walls, against fourteen
milliseconds to draw that same frame.

A game wants something else. It wants to state {e a torch is lit} and get a lit
torch. It wants a door that tracks whether it is open, an enemy that tracks
where it is going, a fuse that tracks how much is left. Each of those should be
written next to the thing it belongs to, not in one function that advances all
of them together.

Written against the platform directly, a game is the second thing forced into
the first:

{[
  type t = { phase : phase; left : float; player : Player.t }

  let update state ~dt ~motion ~actions = (* everything, at once *)
  let view state = (* the whole world, from scratch *)
]}

Every moving part of a world lives in one record and one function, because
there is nowhere else to put them. There is no entity system —
{!Camlcast_core.Room.val-sprite} is an inert record with no identity and no
behaviour — so a torch that flickers, a door that opens and an enemy that walks
are all branches of the same [update].

React solved this for documents, and the solution is not the virtual DOM. It is
this: {b let the program describe what should exist, and keep the state
somewhere the description does not have to mention.} The description is thrown
away every frame. A tree beside it survives, and the mechanism is matching the
two up.

{1 Phase 1 — What a description is}

Start with the type. A description is a tree with four shapes:

{[
  type 'prim t =
    | Empty
    | Fragment of 'prim t list
    | Prim of { prim : 'prim; key : string option; children : 'prim t list }
    | Component : {
        render : 'props -> 'prim t;
        props : 'props;
        key : string option;
        name : string;
      }
        -> 'prim t
]}

Three properties matter.

{b It is parameterised over ['prim].} A description does not reference the
wall types. This lets the reconciler be tested against a host made of strings,
with no renderer linked in, so the hard part of the engine is checked as text
rather than as a picture.

{b [Component] is a GADT constructor.} ['props] appears only inside it, so it
is existentially quantified: a parent can hold children of many different prop
types in one list, and only the component itself sees its own. Without this the
type would have to be parameterised over every prop type in the tree.

{b Nothing is called yet.} [Component] holds a function and its argument. The
description of a subtree is built lazily, one level at a time, as the walk
enters it.

{2 What identifies a component}

Two descriptions at the same place are the same component when their [render]
functions are {e physically the same closure}. A function is the only identity
a component has.

That has a consequence a user must know. A component built inside another
render is a fresh closure every frame. It is therefore never the same component
as last frame, and is torn down and rebuilt — along with everything under it —
sixty times a second. Its state will appear not to work.

{[
  let declare ~name render ?key props = Component { render; props; key; name }
]}

[declare] exists to make the right thing the easy thing: the closure it
captures is made once, when the module is initialised.

{1 Phase 2 — The tree that survives}

Beside the description, an instance tree with the same shape:

{[
  type instance =
    | Nothing
    | Fragment of { path : Path.t; children : instance list }
    | Primitive of { path : Path.t; prim : H.prim; key : string option;
                     children : instance list }
    | Component of { path : Path.t; render_id : Obj.t; key : string option;
                     name : string; slots : Hook.slots; child : instance }
]}

Reconciling walks the two together. At each place it checks whether the thing
described is the thing already here. Same component (same closure, same key) or
same primitive (same key) means keep the instance and walk into it. Anything
else means unmount the old subtree deepest-first and mount the new one.

{2 The one [Obj] you cannot avoid, and the one you can}

[render_id] is an [Obj.t]. Two components' [render] functions have different
types whenever their props differ, so [==] applied to them directly would not
typecheck. [Obj.repr] erases the type and nothing else; physical equality on
the result asks exactly the intended question: are these the same closure?
Nothing is ever cast back, so nothing is assumed about what is behind the
pointer. This is the safe kind of [Obj].

The unsafe kind arrives in Phase 4.

{2 Keys}

Among one parent's children, a keyed description is matched to last frame's by
its key wherever it has moved to, and an unkeyed one by its position. That is
React's rule, for React's reason: a list of enemies that sorts itself by
distance keeps each enemy's state only if the enemies are keyed.

One implementation detail matters. Old children go into an array; keyed ones
are indexed by key; claiming one empties its slot rather than removing it.
Emptying rather than removing keeps the {e leftovers} — the children nothing
claimed, which are about to be unmounted — in the order they were declared. A
hashtable walked at the end would unmount them in unspecified order, and a
trace of a frame would not read the same way twice.

{2 Say what you did}

Reconciling is the part of the engine that is hardest to verify and easiest to
get subtly wrong. A component torn down when it should have been kept still
looks right on screen; the bug only shows later as state that will not persist.

So the reconciler reports what it did — mounted, updated, unmounted, with a
path — and the suite asserts on that:

{v
update   #0 : room
update   #0/torch[two]
update   #0/torch[two]/#0 : flame
update   #0/torch[one]
update   #0/torch[one]/#0 : flame
v}

That is a list of two torches written in the other order, with nothing
remounted. A picture cannot verify that claim; the trace can.

{1 Phase 3 — Paths}

A path is the chain of steps from the root to a node, and it serves two roles.

It is an {b identity}: keyed steps by key, unkeyed steps by index, names
ignored throughout. The reconciler matches on it, and everything hanging off an
instance is found by it.

It is also a {b label}: when a level fails a check, the report must say which
part of the game wrote the offending geometry. [plaza / gallery_wall] is an
answer someone can act on; [World.make: threshold linked twice: plaza.east] is
not.

So a path prints two ways. The friendly form drops anonymous steps, because an
unkeyed index identifies nothing a reader can act on. The debug form keeps
them, because a trace has to tell two sibling walls apart.

{1 Phase 4 — Hooks, on effect handlers}

A component is a function called afresh every frame, so anything it wants to
remember has to live somewhere else. That somewhere is a row of slots on its
instance, and a hook is a request for the next one.

A plain function reaches them through OCaml 5 effect handlers:

{[
  type _ Effect.t +=
    | Use_state : 'a -> ('a * ('a -> unit)) Effect.t
    | Use_ref : 'a -> 'a ref Effect.t
    | Use_memo : { compute : unit -> 'a; deps : 'd; equal : 'd -> 'd -> bool }
        -> 'a Effect.t
    | Use_effect : { start : unit -> (unit -> unit) option; deps : 'd;
                     equal : 'd -> 'd -> bool }
        -> unit Effect.t
]}

[use_state] performs an effect. The one place a component's function is called
installs a handler for it. That is the whole mechanism. It is why a game writes

{[
  let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state true in
]}

in an ordinary function with an ordinary type, with no plumbing in the
signature. A state monad would have put the plumbing in the signature; a
context argument would have put it in every call site.

{2 The slot table, and the cast}

A row of slots is heterogeneous, and OCaml has no way to express that except
[Obj.t]. The cast back is sound exactly when the hook rule holds: the same call
site reaches the same slot every time, so the type it stored is the type it
reads.

A tag on each cell enforces most of that. Three of the four ways to break the
rule become an exception rather than a bad cast:

- a [use_ref] where a [use_state] used to be — the tags differ;
- a render asking for more hooks than last time — the row has settled;
- a render stopping early and asking for fewer — the cursor did not reach the
  end.

The fourth gets through: a conditional swapping one [use_state] for another
[use_state] of a {e different type} at the same index. Same kind, same slot,
different type. That is unsound rather than merely wrong, and {!Camlcast.Hook}
documents it as unsound rather than glossing it. Do not write conditional
hooks.

{2 When a setter takes effect}

React re-renders until the tree settles. This runtime does not: a setter writes
its slot, marks the root, and the change shows up on the next frame.

The trade is explicit. It costs sixteen milliseconds of latency at sixty frames
a second, which has not been noticeable in practice. It buys the impossibility
of a render loop that does not converge, so that class of bug cannot occur. A
game renders every frame anyway; a document does not, which is why React cannot
make the same trade.

{2 Effects run after the frame}

[use_effect]'s work is queued, not run. It happens after the scene is
assembled, with every cleanup before any setup, so a component leaving and a
component arriving in the same frame never overlap on whatever they both hold.
Unmounting queues its cleanups into that same flush rather than running them in
place; that is what makes the ordering hold across a teardown as well as an
update.

Two consequences follow from queueing, and both are implemented explicitly.

A frame that is never assembled runs no effects. [assemble] is the last thing
that can refuse a description — the host reads a forest it did not build and
may find it is not a world — and until it has returned there is nothing to
commit. So the tree is put in place after the scene comes back, not before. A
render that raises throws its queued work away instead of leaving it for the
next frame to flush; otherwise an effect belonging to a rejected description
would start later, under a component that never existed.

That does not make the failed frame unobservable. The components ran: a ref one
of them wrote is written, a memo it recomputed is recomputed, and the trace has
already reported as much. Rolling those back would mean handing every component
a copy of its own slots and swapping the copies in at the end. A [use_ref]
hands out a box by identity, so the one thing a component is most likely to
have written is exactly the thing a copy cannot take back. The tree and the
effects are what the runtime owns, and they are what it rolls back. The rest
belongs to the component, and the interface states that rather than promising a
transaction it cannot keep.

A root that is simply dropped runs no cleanups at all. Every cleanup this
runtime runs is reached through a later render that removed the component, so a
root that is let go of — a one-shot [Mount.build], a checker's forest, a run
whose window has closed — runs none of them, and whatever its effects took
stays taken. {!Camlcast.Mount.destroy} is the missing half of [create]: it
unmounts the tree deepest-first into the same flush, applying the same ordering
at the end of a mount's life rather than the middle of one.

{1 Phase 5 — Contexts, and a type witness}

Some things every part of a world needs and no part wants as a prop: the
atmosphere, the key bindings in force, the font a label is drawn in.

{[
  type 'a t = { id : 'a Type.Id.t; default : 'a }
  type binding = Binding : 'a t * 'a -> binding

  let rec find : type a. binding list -> a t -> a option =
   fun bindings wanted ->
    match bindings with
    | [] -> None
    | Binding (context, value) :: outer -> (
        match Type.Id.provably_equal context.id wanted.id with
        | Some Type.Equal -> Some value
        | None -> find outer wanted)
]}

[Type.Id.provably_equal] returns a {e proof}, not a boolean. Matching on that
proof is what lets [value] leave the function at the type [wanted] was declared
with. There is no cast here; without the witness the function could not be
written at all. This is stronger than the mechanism in React, which this layer
is modelled on: React's context lookup has no proof to offer.

The contrast with Phase 4 is the useful part. A context is a value the game
creates explicitly, so there is somewhere to hang a witness. A hook has only
its position in a row, and there is no per-call-site value to attach anything
to. Where a witness can be had, it is used; where it cannot, the reason is
documented.

{1 Phase 6 — The seam}

Everything so far knows nothing about walls. The whole of what it needs from a
host is this:

{[
  type 'prim node = { path : Path.t; prim : 'prim; children : 'prim node list }

  module type HOST = sig
    type prim
    type scene
    val assemble : prim node list -> scene
  end
]}

Two types and one function. React's host config is written the other way round
— [createInstance], [appendChild], [removeChild] — because the DOM is a mutable
tree that must be mutated node by node. The world a raycaster draws is an
immutable value assembled whole, and the primitives that go into it are cheap
records. Handing over the finished forest and asking for a scene is both
simpler and a better fit.

It is also what makes the reconciler testable. A mock host is a dozen lines — a
primitive is a string, a scene is an indented tree — and every decision the
reconciler makes becomes readable text. It pays a second time: a second host
pointed at the same description, one that assembles {e nothing} and hands back
the forest, is how the level checker reads a description's structure without
building anything.

{1 Phase 7 — The host for a raycaster}

Now the walls. A primitive is a description of one:

{[
  type t =
    | World of { atmosphere : Atmosphere.t; spawn : string * Vec.t }
    | Room of { name : string; floor : Room.surface; ceiling : Room.ceiling }
    | Wall of { a : Vec.t; b : Vec.t; height : float; material : Material.t;
                reacts : reacts }
    | Decal of Room.decal
    | Threshold of Room.threshold * reacts
    | Sprite of Room.sprite * reacts
    | Camera of camera
    | Cursor
    | Finish
    | Link of { here : string * string; there : string * string }
    | Hud | Rect of ... | Bar of ... | Text of ... | Highlight of ... | ...
]}

A [Wall] carries its endpoints rather than an assembled
{!Camlcast_core.Room.val-wall}, because the decals hung on it arrive as its
children and have to be in hand before [Room.wall] can be called at all. Where
nothing nests, the assembled value is carried directly.

[assemble] folds the forest: rooms collect their walls, thresholds and sprites;
the world collects its rooms and links; and out comes a
{!Camlcast_core.World.t}, built by the same [Room.make] and [World.make] a
hand-written level always used.

{2 Two things that are made impossible rather than checked}

{b Winding.} A room is black from the inside if its walls are wound the wrong
way round, and a doorway wound against its room puts the neighbour behind you
when you walk through. Nothing catches either, nothing wants the reversed
version, and the symptom points nowhere near the cause. So a room's outline is
closed: it measures the signed area of the loop it was given and lays the legs
so the room is inside, and a door cut out of a leg takes that winding too. The
same corners in either order build the same room. The measurement is calibrated
against
{!Camlcast_core.Room.rectangle}, which the engine documents as impossible to
get wrong, so the definition cannot drift from what defines it.

{b Names, not indices.} The player carries a room index across frames, and a
description that grows a room, loses one, or writes its rooms in another order
makes that index mean something else. So the loop keeps the room's {e name},
looks it up in each new world, and carries the pose across with an identity
transform. Names are what a description says; indices are what assembling one
produces.

{1 Phase 8 — What it costs}

Assembling a whole world every frame sounds like something to design around.
Measurement, rather than assumption, shows it is not:

{v
describe five rooms         20 us
draw five rooms         14,088 us
v}

Five rooms and a hundred and forty-five walls is the largest world this engine
has. The layer is a seventh of one percent of the frame it is for.

So there is no memoisation in the layer: no props bailout, no cached room per
instance, none of the machinery a reconciler is expected to carry. The
benchmark was written before any of it, and its result said not to. Every node
still carries a stable path, so caching remains a change to one module if the
result ever changes, and [bench/frame.exe] can be re-run instead of the
argument being repeated.

The other number in that table is the significant one. Drawing five rooms takes
most of a sixty-frame budget on its own, because {!Camlcast_core.Ray.cast}
intersects every wall segment in the room once per screen column and there is
no spatial index. That cost is the platform's own, has nothing to do with the
layer, and is where optimisation work would actually pay.

{1 What to read in the source}

In dependency order, and none of them is long:

- [loom/path.ml] — identity and label in one value.
- [loom/element.ml] — the description type, and [declare].
- [loom/trace.ml] — what the reconciler says it did.
- [loom/hook.ml] — the effects, the slot table, the tag check.
- [loom/context.ml] — [Type.Id], and a proof instead of a cast.
- [loom/store.ml] — a reducer and a subscription, and nothing else.
- [loom/reconcile.ml] — the whole of the matching, in one functor.
- [loom/host.ml] — two types and one function.
- [lib/prim.ml], [lib/host.ml] — the walls.

[loom/] depends on nothing but the standard library, and its dune file states
the reasons at length. That constraint is what makes the mock host possible,
and the mock host is what makes any of this checkable.
